Patch 48 -- the island entry marker is an x86 instruction, so emit it only for x86.

The marker Box86 matches in the guest prologue is an eight-byte multi-byte NOP
whose displacement spells "MGS" plus the entry id. It was written as a bare
`.byte` directive. The same WineD3D sources are also compiled for armhf to build
the native island, and there those eight bytes land in the ARM instruction
stream, immediately after the prologue of every one of the 37 marked functions.

As Thumb they decode as:

    1f0f    subs r7, r1, #4
    0084    lsls r4, r0, #2
    474d    bx   r9          <- branches to whatever r9 holds
    1553    never reached

So every marked function in the ARM build branched to a garbage address before
executing any of its own body. Measured on the device on 2026-08-15: the first
island call, wined3d_release_dc, faulted at native PC 0x195f6fc -- a guest stack
address -- and Box86 reported the guest EIP it had stored before entering the
wrapper, the island bridge's own post-wrapper byte. That is the whole of
"wine: Unhandled illegal instruction at 4002017B". No abort stub was reached
because no function body ever ran.

MGS2_ISLAND_MARK() emits the bytes under #ifdef __i386__ and nothing otherwise.

The i386 code is semantically unchanged, but NOT byte-identical, and the
difference is worth stating exactly because an earlier version of this header
claimed it was. Rebuilding wined3d.dll from the documented recipe and comparing
against the deployed wined3d_p47_island_markers.dll:

  same size, same section sizes, 39 markers with the same ids
  67 differing bytes in 2,917,716
    3   the PE checksum field
   64   all in .text, every one immediately after a `mov [esp+N], imm32`,
        every one with a delta of exactly +24

Those 64 are __LINE__ constants from assert() and the debug macros in static
inline functions in wined3d_private.h. This patch adds exactly 24 lines to that
header -- its own explanatory comment -- so every line number below it moves by
24. No instruction changes, no control flow changes, no marker changes.

p47 therefore stays valid and is not rebuilt; only the ARM objects change.

After this patch the same run reaches "MGS2 island: forbidden call to
WindowFromDC" -- an abort stub firing by name, which is the designed behaviour
and the first time native island code executed its own body on this device.

